            <ArticleTitle>Whether Post-Tonsillectomy Medication Should be Liquid Based or Can be Solid? A Randomised, Single-Blinded, Controlled Trial.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>S179-84</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Tonsillectomy is a common operation for the otolaryngologist. There has been a discrepancy in recommending prescribing a liquid based or solid medication for post-tonsillectomy patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare the pain scores, adverse effects and complications between the post-tonsillectomy patients who were given liquid medication in comparison with patients who were given non-restricted medication.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">Patients with chronic hypertrophic tonsillitis who underwent tonsillectomy were recruited. In the control group, patients were given liquid medication. The experimental group was given a non-restricted form of medication. Pain scores, adverse effects and complications and patient satisfaction data were collected.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Twenty-six patients were enrolled. The pain score difference between the 2 groups at 4 hours was -0.23 (95% CI -1.57 to 1.11, p = 0.73) and 0.15 (95% CI -0.77 to 1.08, p = 0.73) at 72 hours. There was no statistically significant difference between the early and late complications between the control group and the experimental group (p &gt; 0.05).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">There was no statistical difference in the pain scores, adverse effects and complications between groups. There is no necessity to restrict patients to liquid medication.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
